context:
What it holds. Two Parquet files in a Julia artifact named journals, not in the repository:
• journals.parquet: every source in OpenAlex with at least one work, 251,461 in the snapshot of 2026-10-14 (203,247 journals, and repositories, conferences, book series and ebook platforms), with ISSNs, publisher, country, field, works, citations, the 2-year mean citedness, h-index, open access, article charges and the counts of every year;
• norwegian.parquet: the Norwegian Register for Scientific Journals, Series and Publishers, 40,245 rows on 2026-10-20, with each title’s level in every year since 2004. How it is updated. A GitHub workflow runs on the 2nd of every month. When OpenAlex has a new snapshot of its sources, it builds both files (build/build.py), publishes them as a release named after the snapshot’s date, and commits the new Artifacts.toml. Pollis sees a new commit, updates the package, and Julia downloads the new artifact. In Julia, PollisJournals.parquet(), norwegian() and snapshot() give the two files and the date. Licences. OpenAlex’s data is CC0; the Norwegian register is under the Norwegian Licence for Open Government Data (NLOD) 2.0, distributed by HK-dir; the package code is MIT. Impact factors, CiteScores and quartiles are not free to share, so the package never holds them: you import your own (§9.2). The database. PollisDB.build_journals() makes PollisJournals.duckdb:

DATA_PACKAGES lists the four packages, each with its GitHub URL and the file ~/.pollis/julia/environments/v1.13/<Pkg> .commit that records the commit installed. pending() asks GitHub for the latest commit of main (the commits API, 3-second

timeout):

• a package not installed is missing, and all missing ones are added by URL in one Pkg.add;
• a package whose recorded commit differs from GitHub’s is outdated, and Pkg.update fetches it;
• offline, or for a copy you installed in an environment of your own, nothing is done. The packages go into Pollis’s own environment, never into your projects; your active project is left as it was.

A round is a set of works made seeds, then the lists of citations that follow them. Each list of citations keeps the pool’s top as it was after it, and the next round is compared with it: a line per round tells how many works of the top are new, marked ↑ New in the top 20. A round that brings nothing new into the top has saturated the search, and the page says The search has saturated. Run to Saturation runs the rounds itself: it follows the seeds not followed yet, then, round after round, makes the best candidates seeds and follows them, until the search saturates or the rounds run out. Beside it:
• Seeds a round, 5 at first: the candidates made seeds each round;
• Rounds at most, 6 at first;
• Following: Cited By, Cites, or both. They are remembered. While it runs its button reads Stop, which ends the run once the round it is in ends; a list half fetched is never kept, so a step is always whole. Excluding a work keeps Run to Saturation from making it a seed.
